---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { catalogCategories } from '../lib/catalog';
import { priorityCategorySlugs } from '../lib/category-content';
const categoryIcons: Record<string, string> = { 't-shirts': '👕', dresses: '👗', jackets: '🧥', shoes: '👟', heels: '👠', 'trousers-pants': '👖', eyewear: '🕶️', perfume: '🧴', headwear: '🧢', 'underwear-swim': '🩱', electronics: '📱', 'scarves-ties': '🧣', 'jewelry-watches': '💍', 'socks-hosiery': '🧦' };
const categoryLabels: Record<string, string> = { heels: 'High Heels', 'trousers-pants': 'Pants', eyewear: 'Sunglasses', headwear: 'Hats', 'underwear-swim': 'Underwear', 'scarves-ties': 'Scarves', 'jewelry-watches': 'Jewelry', 'socks-hosiery': 'Socks' };
const guideCategories = priorityCategorySlugs.map((slug) => catalogCategories.find((category) => category.slug === slug)).filter(Boolean);
const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is an Oopbuy spreadsheet?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'An Oopbuy spreadsheet is an independent product-discovery resource that groups current-looking finds into categories. It helps users compare listings and QC availability, but it is not a checkout page or a guarantee of stock.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How should I use an Oopbuy spreadsheet?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Start with one focused category, compare a small shortlist, then verify the exact product, size, option, price and availability on the live listing. Review available warehouse QC photos before deciding about shipping.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Are Oopbuy spreadsheet links always current?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'No. Products, variants, prices and availability can change. Treat a spreadsheet card as a starting point and verify the current live listing before taking any action.' } }
  ]
});
---
<BaseLayout title="Oopbuy Spreadsheet: Finds, QC Photos & Buyer Guide | OopbuyTop" description="Use this independent Oopbuy spreadsheet to browse focused finds, compare categories, check available QC photos and verify the current listing before ordering.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Independent Oopbuy spreadsheet · updated 2026</p>
    <h1>Oopbuy Spreadsheet: Find, Compare &amp; Check QC</h1>
    <p>This independent Oopbuy spreadsheet helps you start with a focused category, compare current-looking finds and see when warehouse QC photos are available. It is designed for research: every card should lead to a live-listing check, not an assumption about stock, price or the exact variant.</p>
    <p>Use the spreadsheet when you need a short, comparable set of products instead of a giant mixed list. The practical question behind every card is the same: is this the right item type, does the visible price make sense, are QC photos available, and does the current listing still match what you are looking for?</p>
    <p><strong>Jump to:</strong> <a href="/categories/">Oopbuy spreadsheet categories</a> · <a href="/guides/oopbuy-qc-photos/">Oopbuy QC photo guide</a> · <a href="/guides/oopbuy-size-guide/">size-check guide</a> · <a href="/guides/oopbuy-shipping-guide/">shipping guide</a></p>
    <h2>Step 1: Start with a specific category</h2>
    <p>Choose the product type first. Comparing sneakers with sneakers, or bags with bags, makes differences in title, photos, options and price context easier to spot. Starting from a narrow category also reduces the temptation to save dozens of links you never review.</p>
    <div class="guide-category-grid">{guideCategories.map((category) => category && <a class="guide-category-card" href={`/category/${category.slug}/`}><b aria-hidden="true">{categoryIcons[category.slug]}</b><span>{categoryLabels[category.slug] ?? category.name}</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i></a>)}</div>
    <h2>Step 2: Use the card for context, not a final decision</h2>
    <p>A product card can show a title, a displayed price range, a brand label and the number of available QC photos. Those are useful research signals. They are not a promise that the exact variant is available, that measurements will fit, or that the final price will be unchanged.</p>
    <p>When a card looks relevant, keep it on a small shortlist. Compare it with two or three similar listings before you spend time on a full review. This is usually more useful than picking the first item with the most attractive photo.</p>
    <h2>Step 3: Open the current listing and verify it</h2>
    <p>The live listing is where you verify the details that matter: the selectable color or size, measurements, seller images, stated materials, price and availability. Listings change often, so an old link or cached card should never override what is currently shown on the destination page.</p>
    <p>Check that the visible option is the one you actually want. A price may belong to a different size, color or configuration. If a detail is unclear, do not assume that a similar listing is identical.</p>
    <h2>Step 4: Review QC before you plan a parcel</h2>
    <p>QC photos are warehouse reference images taken after an item arrives. They can help you compare the received item with the option you selected. Look first at color, labels, construction, obvious marks and completeness. A larger photo count gives you more angles, but it is not a quality guarantee.</p>
    <p>Make the QC decision before an item is included in a shipment. If a key detail is not visible, use the current support options provided by the service you are using rather than guessing from an old image.</p>
    <h2>Step 5: Keep shipping as a separate decision</h2>
    <p>Shipping depends on destination, parcel details and the options visible when you check out. Product discovery cannot promise a shipping price, delivery date or eligibility. After you have reviewed your items, read the current shipping information in the official account you use.</p>
    <h2>Oopbuy spreadsheet checklist</h2>
    <ol><li>Choose one focused category.</li><li>Compare a short set of relevant products.</li><li>Verify the exact live listing, size and variant.</li><li>Review QC photos before approving shipment.</li><li>Use official account pages for payment, support and delivery choices.</li></ol>
    <h2>What this Oopbuy spreadsheet can and cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>It can make discovery faster by keeping comparable product types together and showing the available information in a consistent format. It cannot verify that a listing is still live, that a price belongs to your chosen option, that a product will fit, or that shipping will be offered to your destination. Those are decisions to make only after you open the current listing and your own account’s live checkout.</p>
    <p>For that reason, prioritise cards with clear titles, relevant images and warehouse QC availability, then compare no more than a few candidates at a time. If you need a route by product type, start with the <a href="/categories/">Oopbuy spreadsheet categories</a>. If you already have an item at a warehouse, use the <a href="/guides/oopbuy-qc-photos/">QC photo checklist</a> before planning a parcel.</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Does a spreadsheet sell the product?</h3><p>No. It is a discovery resource. Orders, payment and support belong on the official service you choose.</p>
    <h3>Can I rely on the displayed price?</h3><p>Use it as comparison context only. Confirm the current price and exact option on the live listing.</p>
    <h3>Are Oopbuy spreadsheet links always current?</h3><p>No. Products, variants, prices and availability can change. Use a card as a starting point, then verify the live listing before taking action.</p>
    <p><a class="button button-hot" href="/categories/">Browse Oopbuy spreadsheet categories →</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqSchema} />
